※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ `⊥` is a result that has no answer, written out, and `⊥ VALUE` is one whose
※ error carries that value.  Until now the only way to make one was an
※ operation that had no answer for what it was given -- a division by zero, a
※ divisor that turned out to be zero -- so a program could read a failure and
※ not produce one.
※
※ **What it is a failure of is what stands where it stands.**  That is the same
※ rule a value of the answer type follows: `0u8` written where a `u8?` is
※ wanted is the successful result of that type, and `⊥` written there is the
※ failure of it.  So nothing is written beside the glyph to say which result it
※ is, and there is no reading of it anywhere a result is not wanted (4522).
※
※ Whether it carries a value is the type's to say and not the program's: an
※ error written `TYPE?` is the fact that there is no answer and nothing more,
※ and one written `TYPE?ERROR` is that fact and a value beside it.
※
※ It takes the whole of what follows it, the way `return` does, so `⊥ a + b`
※ carries the sum and there is no reading in which `⊥ a` is the left side of
※ anything.

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let plain: u8? = nothing()
    let carried: u8?u16 = saying(700u16)
    let summed: u8?u16 = adding(300u16, 400u16)
    let held: mut u16 = 0u16
    let also: mut u16 = 0u16
    match carried:
        u8:
            held ← 1u16
        ⊥(why):
            held ← why
    match summed:
        u8:
            also ← 1u16
        ⊥(why):
            also ← why
    ※ an error that carries nothing is still an error, and `??` reads it
    if (plain ?? 9u8) ≠ 9u8 ∨ (answered(false) ?? 9u8) ≠ 3u8:
        1u6
    elif (answered(true) ?? 9u8) ≠ 9u8:
        2u6
    ※ and one that carries something hands it to whoever matches
    elif held ≠ 700u16:
        3u6
    ※ what follows the glyph is the whole of it, so this carried the sum
    elif also ≠ 700u16:
        4u6
    ※ a failure written in one arm of an `if` and an answer in the other, which
    ※ is the same type either way
    elif (either(true) ?? 9u8) ≠ 9u8 ∨ (either(false) ?? 9u8) ≠ 4u8:
        5u6
    ※ and one propagated with `?`, carrying what it carried
    elif passed(700u16) ≠ 700u16:
        6u6
    else:
        0u6

fn nothing() → u8?:
    ⊥

fn saying(why: u16) → u8?u16:
    ⊥ why

fn adding(a: u16, b: u16) → u8?u16:
    ⊥ a + b

fn answered(fail: bool) → u8?:
    if fail:
        ⊥
    else:
        3u8

fn either(fail: bool) → u8?:
    if fail:
        ⊥
    else:
        4u8

fn passed(why: u16) → u16:
    match carrying(why):
        u8:
            0u16
        ⊥(number):
            number

fn carrying(why: u16) → u8?u16:
    let v: u8 = saying(why)?
    v
